{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>My dwelling.</b> My dwelling, like מדור באהלי רשע (Psalms 84:11). This expression is known in Aramaic. It is also correct that דור and דור are from its root, in the sense of כי גר אנכי עמך (Psalms 39:13)." "<b>נסע.</b> From the nifal binyan; the samekh received a dagesh because of the loss of the nun, which is the first root letter." "<b>נגלה.</b> From the root of גלות (Isaiah 20:4)." "<b>מני.</b> Like ממני, and likewise שעו מני (Isaiah 22:4)." "<b>Like a shepherd's tent.</b> The yod is for the construct state of the word, or it is an internal yod (1 Kings 6:27), also אכזרי [Isaiah 13:18] — for a shepherd's tent does not stand in one place." "<b>I have rolled up.</b> Like 'I have folded up.'" "<b>[Like a weaver].</b> And similarly: וימי קלו מני ארג (Job 7:6)." "<b>מדלה.</b> The illness, like מדוע [אתה] ככה דל בן המלך (2 Samuel 13:4)." "<b>[מדלה יבצעני].</b> For from this illness He will cut me off. יתמו שני [his two years?] — like יתר ידו ויבצעני (Job 6:9)." "<b>[From day etc.].b> And this illness — which makes with me peace by day and war by night — and thus most illnesses do. And behold, תשלימני, like ואם לא תשלים (Deuteronomy 20:12)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {8 "The phrase יתמו שני is textually difficult; translated hyper-literally without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:56:56.931720688Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}